    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Town Square</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> Here Is a Map of the Town.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Interpret cultural differences in communication. Concept: Clarify misunderstandings in group discussions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can interpret cultural differences by using simple examples and gestures (e.g., says, "In Panama, people use palm leaves to make roofs." and gestures to a roof or points out a window).
            Can clarify misunderstandings in group discussions by pointing to visuals (e.g., pointing to the map and saying, "The park is here, not there.").
            Can resolve misunderstandings about locations using simple phrases (e.g., saying, "This is the library." and pointing it on a map).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a simple, hand-drawn map of a fictional "PanaTown Square" with a few labeled landmarks (e.g., "market," "fountain," "school," "statue"). The teacher intentionally places one landmark incorrectly or leaves out a detail. Students briefly review the map, identifying familiar places and noticing the missing/incorrect detail. For example, "There is a park, but where is the library?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to clarify a misunderstanding or explain a cultural detail using the map. For example, pointing to the incorrectly placed library, "Oh, the library is *not* here. The library is *across from* the school, *here*." (points correctly). Or, pointing to a drawing of a 'kiosko' and saying, "In Panama, we often have a 'kiosko' in the plaza, like *this one* (gestures to the drawing), which is a small stand. It's a *tradition*." The teacher models clear pronunciation of words like "street" and "statue," emphasizing the initial /s/ sound.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If someone points to the wrong place on the map, what should I say to help them?" (Expected: "No, it's not there. It's here.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I want to explain something special about our town, like a 'traditional roof,' do I just say the word or show an example?" (Expected: "Show an example or use gestures.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Town Square")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio recording or text description (e.g., a "Visitor's Guide to El Dorado Town Square") that includes specific details about its layout and cultural elements. The description will mention landmarks such as "the historic church," "the modern library," a "bustling market," and "a fountain with a statue." It will also describe a unique cultural feature, e.g., "The old buildings here often have *traditional* clay *roofs*."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpret cultural differences in communication.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to the description twice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What are three important places in El Dorado Town Square?" (e.g., church, library, market).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students answer T/F questions based on the description (e.g., "The library is an old building. T/F").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match locations to their descriptions (e.g., "Fountain" -> "has a statue," "Buildings" -> "have traditional roofs").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Grammar: Imperatives, sequencing words, There is/there are, WH-questions. Vocabulary: town square, map, statue, monument, plaza, intersection, fountain, street, building, roof, tradition, park, library, market, school, crosswalk, traffic light, sidewalk, town hall, pedestrian bridge, locate, visit, walk, turn, cross, gather, pass, follow, point, describe, historic, modern, busy, quiet, traditional, spacious, small, tall, crowded, this, that, these, those, near, between, across from, beside, next to, behind, in front of, under, over, on, at, on the corner of, here, there, nearby, around, ahead, straight. Pronunciation: /s/ clusters in "street," syllable stress in "monument," "tradition."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: "Fix the Map"</b> (5 min)
                        <br>The teacher provides students with an incomplete or slightly incorrect map of a town square (similar to the warm-up, but more detailed). Students are given a list of sentences describing locations, some of which are false according to their map. In pairs, students must correct the false statements using "No, there isn't..." or "No, it's not..." and then write the correct location using "There is/are..." and prepositions.
                        <br><i>Example: "The library is next to the park." (Map shows library across from park). Student response: "No, the library is not next to the park. The library is across from the park."</i></li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: "Clarify the Directions"</b> (8 min)
                        <br>Students work in pairs. Partner A has a map and Partner B has a set of directions to a specific place (e.g., "Go straight, turn left at the monument, pass the school, the library is on your right."). Partner B reads the directions aloud. Partner A follows on their map, but intentionally gets confused or pretends to. Partner B must then clarify the directions using sequencing words, imperatives, prepositions, demonstratives ("this," "that"), and gestures.
                        <br><i>Example: Partner A: "So, I turn left at the fountain?" Partner B: "No, *that's* not right. *First*, go straight, *then* turn left at the *monument*, *here*." (points to monument on map). Teacher circulates, monitoring use of /s/ clusters in words like "straight," "street."</i></li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction/Peer Check: "Cultural Interpretation"</b> (7 min)
                        <br>The teacher shows pairs two pictures: one of a generic park bench, and another of a "taburete" or a typical Panamanian handmade chair often found in rural areas or crafts. Students, working together, must describe the Panamanian item and explain any cultural difference, mediating the information to their partner. They can use phrases like "In Panama, people use..." or "This is similar to X, but it's a *tradition* to make it *this way*." Partners provide feedback on clarity and understanding.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Town Square")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small group "Town Photo Tour" Presentation and Clarification.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students, in groups of 3-4, present 2-3 photos from their "Town Photo Tour" project (which they would have prepared in previous lessons). These photos depict landmarks, buildings, or interesting features from their town square or school's central area. Each group presents their photos, taking turns to describe locations using "there is/are" and prepositions of place (e.g., "There is a *historic* monument *in front of* the town hall."). As they present, other groups act as "visitors" and can ask questions (e.g., "Where is that *fountain*?" or "What is *that building* *behind* the *park*?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The presenting group must actively mediate any misunderstandings or clarify information. They should use demonstratives ("this," "that"), point to visuals, use gestures, and employ simple phrases to resolve confusion or interpret cultural aspects (e.g., "This is a *traditional* *roof* made of palm leaves, common *here*," or "No, the *market* is not *next to* the *school*; it's *across from* it. See *this* street *here*?"). The teacher encourages students to pronounce two- and three-syllable words and compound nouns clearly, paying attention to stress (e.g., "monument," "traffic light").</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in the "Town Photo Tour" group presentation, focusing on their ability to mediate and clarify information for their peers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of explanations and clarifications (e.g., "The park is here, not there.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of "there is/are" and prepositions of place to describe locations.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of gestures and pointing to aid understanding.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effort to interpret cultural differences using simple examples (e.g., "In Panama, people use palm leaves to make roofs.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of target vocabulary, especially /s/ clusters and syllable stress in multi-syllable words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborative interaction and active participation in group discussions.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well did I explain the locations and resolve misunderstandings during my group's photo tour presentation today?" (Circle: Very well / Well / A little / Not yet)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new word or phrase I used today to clarify something about our town square?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about clarifying information about the town photos for my classmates or visitors?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What strategies did I use today (e.g., pointing, gesturing, simple phrases) to help my classmates understand better when there was a misunderstanding?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students mediate information and clarify misunderstandings about the town square photos and cultural aspects? (e.g., specific activities, types of input, partner work).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds might be needed for students to interpret cultural differences more effectively in future mediation tasks, especially for students who struggled? (e.g., more visual aids, simpler sentence structures for explanations).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "Here Is a Map of the Town" theme by consolidating all language and skills through the Town Photo Tour project. The mediation skills practiced today, such as clarifying misunderstandings and interpreting cultural information, are crucial for effective communication and will be applicable to all future communicative tasks and projects in upcoming themes.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>